Step 2 — Find the Injectable Parameter
The challenge presents us with a standard search interface. Whenever you see a search box in a CTF, your first instinct should be to test for SQL Injection (SQLi).

I started by throwing standard boolean-based payloads into the search term field to see how the backend database would react.

The classic payload worked perfectly:

' OR 1=1 --
This successfully bypassed the search filter and dumped the contents of the default table. However, instead of the real flag, the database spit out a massive list of flags:

flag1: picoCTF{n0T_F0uNd_s3cr3T_K3y_34rd76s1}
malicious: picoCTF{tH15_lS_n0T_f!@G_5rwdf731q}
…and several others.
We were in the right database, but we were looking at the wrong table.

Step 3 — Run SQLMap (Union Injection) To Dump Database
Since the default table (flags) was full of decoys, I needed to look around the rest of the SQLite database. After enumerating the schema, I discovered another table named users.

