To extract the data without crashing the server (which can happen if you use heavy automated tools like sqlmap), I crafted a manual UNION-based SQL Injection.

Since I wanted to see all the usernames and passwords on the screen at once, I used GROUP_CONCAT to glue them together into a single string.

Here is the exact payload used in the search box:

' UNION SELECT 1, GROUP_CONCAT(username || ': ' || password) FROM users --
The database returned a beautiful list of internal users and their passwords: admin: 5a9a79d9fa477ed163b89088681672c9, ctf-player: 7a67ab5872843b22b5e14511867c4e43, and many more ...

Step 4— Crack The MD5 Hash
Looking at the output, the passwords were clearly 32-character hexadecimal strings. This is exactly what the challenge description meant by “legacy hashing practices” — these are MD5 hashes.

Our target was the ctf-player account:

Username: ctf-player
Hash: 7a67ab5872843b22b5e14511867c4e43
